      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & =\\frac{(4)^{2}-16}{(4)^{2}-4(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-16}{16-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & =\\frac{(y-4)(y+4)}{y(y-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+4}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{{y}^{2}-16}{{y}^{2}-4y} & ={lim}_{y\\to 4}\\frac{y+4}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
